#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# centered_interval

#% text
# `centered_interval(c, r)` represents the interval of the numbers within `r` of `c`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Functions give f(c) with the radius r times the largest |f'| at c - r, c and c + r (an estimate, not a rigorous enclosure; balls give those).

#% item
# `centered_interval([[m, e, rm, re], p])` reads the centre m 2^e and the radius rm 2^re.

#% item
# `centered_interval(x)` of an exact number or a numeric constant gives a machine centre and a radius rounded up so that the interval holds x (a box for a complex x); of `interval([a, b])` it gives the midpoint and the half-width the same way. With two arguments that are not both machine numbers the centre becomes the nearest machine number and the radius a machine number rounded up, with the rounding of the centre added, so that the interval holds the one given (a centre and a radius that machine numbers hold exactly, as 2 and 1/2, are kept as they are); a machine number alone gets the radius |x| eps (the numbers that round to it and to its neighbours).

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
centered_interval(2, 0.1)
#% output
#= centered_interval(2.0, 0.1)

#% cell
airy_ai(centered_interval(2, 0.1))
#% output
#= centered_interval(0.03527351107369801, 0.005320908957831578)

#% cell
centered_interval(1, 1/10) + 2
#% output
#= centered_interval(3.0, 0.10000000000000617)

#% cell
centered_interval(1/3)
#% output
#= centered_interval(0.3333333333333333, 3.7007434154171913e-17)

#% cell
centered_interval(interval([1, 2]))
#% output
#= centered_interval(1.5, 0.5000000000000014)

